The '''1999 UEFA Champions League final''' was an [[association football]] match between [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] of England and [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] of Germany, played at [[Camp Nou]] in [[Barcelona]], Spain, on 26 May 1999, to determine the winner of the [[1998–99 UEFA Champions League]]. [[Association football#Duration and tie-breaking methods|Injury time]] goals from Manchester United's [[Teddy Sheringham]] and [[Ole Gunnar Solskjær]] cancelled out [[Mario Basler]]'s early goal for Bayern to give Manchester United a 2–1 win and their first European Cup title since [[1968 European Cup final|1968]], their second overall. Referee [[Pierluigi Collina]] has cited this match as one of the most memorable of his career, and described the noise from the crowd at the end of the game as being like a "lion's roar".

The two sides had played each other earlier in the competition, having both been drawn in Group D in the group stage; Bayern won the group, while Manchester United qualified for the knockout phase as one of the two best runners-up across all six groups. After beating [[Inter Milan]] in the quarter-finals, Manchester United beat another Italian side, [[Juventus FC|Juventus]], in the semis to reach the final; meanwhile, Bayern beat fellow Germans [[1. FC Kaiserslautern]] in the quarter-finals, before overcoming Ukrainian side [[FC Dynamo Kyiv|Dynamo Kyiv]] in the semis. The victory completed a [[1998–99 Manchester United F.C. season|treble-winning season]] for Manchester United, having already secured the [[1998–99 FA Premier League|Premier League]] and [[1998–99 FA Cup|FA Cup]] titles in the preceding 10 days. Bayern were also playing for a treble, having won the [[1998–99 Bundesliga|Bundesliga]] and reached the [[1998–99 DFB-Pokal|DFB-Pokal]] final, although they went on to lose [[1999 DFB-Pokal Final|that match]] as well to [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] on penalties. It has been widely regarded as one of the greatest finals in the history of the tournament.

==Background==
[[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] and [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] had only met twice in competitive matches before the final, both meetings coming earlier in the 1998–99 season and both finished as draws.<ref name="head_to_head">{{cite web |title=United versus Bayern Munich |url=http://www.stretfordend.co.uk/opponents/bayernmunich.html |publisher=StretfordEnd.co.uk |access-date=14 January 2014}}</ref> Manchester United's only other German opponents in their history were [[Borussia Dortmund]], against whom they had an overall winning record, with three wins, two defeats and a draw in their six matches, including a 10–1 aggregate win in the second round of the [[1964–65 Inter-Cities Fairs Cup]] and a 2–0 aggregate defeat in the [[1996–97 UEFA Champions League]] semi-finals.<ref>{{cite news |title=The record: Germany v Manchester United |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/special_report/1999/05/99/uniteds_euro_showdown/347099.stm |work=BBC News |date=19 May 1999 |access-date=14 January 2014}}</ref> Bayern Munich had an equally narrow advantage in their 22 matches against English opposition, with seven wins, nine draws and six defeats, including a win over [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]] in the [[1975 European Cup final]] and a defeat to [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] in the [[1982 European Cup final|1982 final]].<ref>{{cite news |title=The record: England v Bayern Munich |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/special_report/1999/05/99/uniteds_euro_showdown/347096.stm |work=BBC News |date=19 May 1999 |access-date=14 January 2014}}</ref>

Including the victory over Leeds in 1975, Bayern Munich had won the European Cup on three occasions going into the 1999 final. With three victories in a row from [[1974 European Cup final|1974]] to [[1976 European Cup final|1976]],<ref>{{cite news |date=12 May 1976 |title=1975/76: Roth completes Bayern hat-trick |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0cda61c4e92c-4a5416e93b00-1000--1975-76-roth-completes-bayern-hat-trick/|access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> they became only the third team to achieve such a feat after [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] ([[1956 European Cup final|1956]] to [[1960 European Cup final|1960]])<ref>{{cite news |date=18 May 1960 |title=1959/60: Dazzling Madrid crush Frankfurt |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d7b1dc17e3e-c896eaba1732-1000--1959-60-dazzling-madrid-crush-frankfurt/|access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> and [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] ([[1971 European Cup final|1971]] to [[1973 European Cup final|1973]]).<ref>{{cite news |date=30 May 1973 |title=1972/73: Rep makes it three for Ajax |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0029-0e6a067551a7-400ba0a1c6f9-1000--1972-73-rep-makes-it-three-for-ajax/|access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> They had also finished as runners-up twice: in 1982 against Aston Villa<ref>{{cite news |date=26 May 1982 |title=1981/82: Withe brings Villa glory |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0254-0d7b1e154752-f2cfb521c62d-1000--1981-82-withe-brings-villa-glory/|access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> and [[1987 European Cup final|1987]] against [[FC Porto|Porto]].<ref>{{cite news |date=27 May 1987 |title=1986/87: Madjer inspires Porto triumph |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0cd4291ecca1-29e5116479e2-1000/|access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> Although Bayern had been waiting 23 years for a European Cup title, Manchester United had had to wait even longer, as their only victory had come in [[1968 European Cup final|1968]] against [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] at Wembley in extra time.<ref>{{cite news |title=1968: Manchester Utd win European Cup |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/may/29/newsid_4464000/4464446.stm |publisher=BBC |work=BBC On This Day |access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> Their manager then was [[Matt Busby]], who had been seriously injured 10 years earlier in the [[Munich air disaster]], which killed eight of his players on the way back from a European Cup tie in Belgrade, before rebuilding the team to become European Cup winners. Busby died in 1994; the day of the 1999 Champions League final would have been his 90th birthday.<ref name="treble_joy">{{cite news |title=Treble joy for United fans |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/353844.stm |work=BBC News |date=27 May 1999 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref>

Although it was the second Champions League season to feature clubs that had not won their national leagues the year before, Manchester United and Bayern Munich were the first such clubs to reach the final of the competition.<ref name="Ferris">{{cite book |last1=Ferris |first1=Ken |title=Manchester United in Europe: Tragedy, Destiny, History |edition=2nd |year=2004 |orig-year=2001 |publisher=Mainstream Publishing |location=Edinburgh |isbn=1-84018-897-9 |page=339}}</ref>{{rp|p=354}} Nevertheless, both went into the match as champions, having won their domestic leagues in 1998–99; Bayern Munich claimed the [[Bundesliga]] title with a 1–1 draw against [[Hertha BSC]] on 9 May with three games to go, while Manchester United left it until the last day of the season (16 May), when they came back from 1–0 down against [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] to win 2–1 and beat [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] to the title by a point.<ref>{{cite news |title=Glorious United crowned champions |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/fa_carling_premiership/345445.stm |work=BBC News |date=27 May 1999 |access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> Both teams were also playing for the [[Treble (association football)|treble]]; in addition to their league win, Manchester United had beaten [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] in the [[1999 FA Cup Final]] on 22 May to claim the [[Double (association football)|Double]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Double joy for Man United |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/350192.stm |work=BBC News |date=22 May 1999 |access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> while Bayern were due to play [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] in the [[1998–99 DFB-Pokal|1999 DFB-Pokal Final]] on 12 June.<ref name="sky_treble">{{cite news |first=Peter |last=Fraser |title=Treble of 1999 |url=http://www.skysports.com/football/news/11946/8620802/treble-of-1999 |work=Sky Sports (BSkyB) |date=28 January 2014 |access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref>

