==Post-match and legacy==
{{Quote box |quoted=true |bgcolor=#FFFFF0 |salign=center
|width=100%
|align=center
|quote=…For me, it was as though the world stood still. I thought, "This is – this is what paradise is all about. It's got to be." And I honestly do not remember what I did for about five minutes…And suddenly, the world was great again. And I thought, "Well, this…There is nothing ever going to be better than this."
|source=[[Bobby Charlton]], 1966 World Cup winner with England and United's 1968 European Cup-winning captain who watched the final live at the Camp Nou, recalling his reaction to Solskjær's winner in an interview<ref>{{cite web |title=Sir Bobby Charlton perfectly sums up why we're all football fans |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3chqy_zdF_A |website=YouTube |date=21 October 2023 |publisher=The Independent |access-date=27 October 2023 |language=en}}</ref>
}}
[[File:1999 UEFA Champions League celebration (edited).jpg|thumb|Manchester United's players celebrate after the game.]]

The game's ending was so unexpected that [[UEFA]] President [[Lennart Johansson]] had left his seat in the stands before Sheringham's equaliser to make his way down to the pitch to present the [[European Champion Clubs' Cup|European Cup trophy]], which was already decorated with red and white ribbons – the colours of both teams.<ref name="Harris">{{cite book |last1=Harris |first1=Daniel |title=The Promised Land |year=2013 |publisher=Arena Sport |location=Edinburgh |isbn=978-1-909715-05-9}}</ref>{{rp|p=261}}<ref>{{cite web |title=1998/99 Manchester United FC 2-1 FC Bayern München: Report |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e8-0e6a21aa8a51-871133889c58-1000--1998-99-manchester-united-fc-2-1-fc-bayern-munchen-report/ |website=UEFA |date=1 August 2010 |publisher=UEFA |access-date=19 March 2026 |language=en}}</ref> When emerging from the tunnel at the final whistle, he was stunned, and he later said: "I can't believe it. The winners are crying and the losers are dancing."<ref name="Harris"/>{{rp|p=272}} When the two teams went to collect their medals, Matthäus received his runner-up medal but removed it from his neck almost immediately.<ref>{{cite news |first=John |last=Brewin |title=Lothar Matthäus, unsurpassed but unloved |url=http://www.espnfc.co.uk/story/629453/lothar-matth%C3%A3%C2%A4us-unsurpassed-but-unloved |work=ESPN FC |date=20 March 2009 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> He never won the competition as a player, having moved to play in the United States for the [[New York Red Bulls|MetroStars]] in March 2000, the year before Bayern next won the European Cup in [[2001 UEFA Champions League final|2001]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Majid |last=Mohamed |title=The best not to have won the Champions League |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0221-0e90c7564eb5-3b70d5ac744b-1000--the-best-not-to-have-won-the-champions-league/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=19 May 2015 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> Matthäus later commented that "it was not the best team that won but the luckiest".<ref>{{cite news |title=Matthaus: United were lucky |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/353876.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=26 May 1999 |access-date=3 January 2012}}</ref> As captain of Manchester United, Schmeichel should have received his medal last, but instead he went up first before having to return to the back of the queue with Ferguson; the pair then lifted the trophy together, surrounded by the rest of Manchester United's players, including May, who climbed the presentation podium to a position of prominence in the celebration photographs.<ref name="Ferris"/>{{rp|p=401}}

Staying true to his word from earlier in the season, the match was Schmeichel's last in a Manchester United shirt, and a month later he signed for Portuguese club [[Sporting CP]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Jon |last=Brodkin |title=Schmeichel takes his leave in Sporting fashion |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1999/jun/21/newsstory.sportinglisbon |work=The Guardian |date=21 June 1999 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> He spent two seasons in Portugal before returning to England for spells with Aston Villa and [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Villa clinch Schmeichel deal |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/a/aston_villa/1435755.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=12 July 2001 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Man City snap up Schmeichel |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/a/aston_villa/1926287.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=13 April 2002 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> It was also Blomqvist's last competitive appearance for the club. After playing in all four matches of the club's pre-season tour of Australia and China in July 1999, he suffered a succession of knee injuries that ruled him out of competitive action for the next two seasons. He made two more appearances for the first team during the 2000–01 pre-season campaign, as well as three matches for the reserves in September and October 2001, before a free transfer to [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] in November that year.<ref>{{cite book |editor1-last=Ponting |editor1-first=Ivan |title=Match of My Life: Manchester United |year=2007 |publisher=Know The Score Books |location=Studley |isbn=978-1-905449-59-0 |chapter=Jesper Blomqvist v Bayern Munich, UEFA Champions League final, May 1999 |first=Jesper |last=Blomqvist |pages=192–3}}</ref> For Bayern Munich, substitutes Helmer and [[Ali Daei]] left the club in the summer of 1999; Helmer moved to England to play for [[Sunderland A.F.C.|Sunderland]], while Daei remained in Germany with Hertha BSC.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wearsiders capture German Helmer |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/393276.stm |work=BBC News |date=13 July 1999 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Hertha BSC: Ali Daei der neue "Held von Berlin" |trans-title=Hertha BSC: Ali Daei, the new "Hero of Berlin" |url=http://www.spiegel.de/sport/fussball/hertha-bsc-ali-daei-der-neue-held-von-berlin-a-42915.html |website=spiegel.de |publisher=Spiegel Online |date=22 September 1999 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref>

Bayern president [[Franz Beckenbauer]] recognised that it was a loss of concentration in the last few minutes that led to his side's defeat: "That was the cruellest defeat possible because victory was so close. We already had victory in the bag and there were only a few seconds to go. But those few seconds were enough for Manchester [United] to get back into the game. We were playing well in the last 20 minutes and should have been able to hold on to victory. But in the end, Manchester deservedly won."<ref name="quotes">{{cite news |title=Barcelona: Quotes at a glance |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/353901.stm |work=BBC News |date=27 May 1999 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> Manager Hitzfeld seemed more stunned by the result in his reaction: "I feel so sorry for my team because they were so close to winning this match but they have lost. It's really difficult to digest and this is inconceivable for us but then this is what football is all about. Normally when the opposition equalise you are expecting extra-time and it was a shock to our team when they scored the winner two minutes later. It could take days or even weeks to recover from this but I must say that Manchester are great champions."<ref name="quotes"/>

[[File:The Champions League Winners Medal (Manchester United Museum) (262769292).jpg|thumb|A winner's medal from the 1999 Champions League final on display in the Manchester United Museum]]

In winning the trophy, Manchester United became the first English team to be crowned European champions since the [[Heysel Stadium disaster]] ban in 1985, which had resulted in English clubs being excluded from UEFA competitions for five years, and also the first team to achieve the treble of the [[Premier League]], [[FA Cup]], and [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] in the same season.<ref>{{cite news |first=Martin |last=Thorpe |title=Solskjaer takes Treble chance |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1999/may/26/newsstory.sport |work=The Guardian |location=London |date=26 May 1999 |access-date=30 January 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090217094902/http://www.guardian.co.uk/football/1999/may/26/newsstory.sport |archive-date=17 February 2009 |url-status=live}}</ref> It was the first time the treble had been won since [[PSV Eindhoven]] in 1988; it would not be won again until Barcelona beat Manchester United 10 years later in the [[2009 UEFA Champions League final]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Inter join exclusive treble club |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0250-0c50f4b42c3a-0fbc1831ccbe-1000--inter-join-exclusive-treble-club/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=22 May 2010 |access-date=21 August 2015}}</ref> After becoming the first manager of an English club to achieve this honour, Ferguson was made a [[Knight Bachelor]] on 12 June 1999 in recognition of his services to football,<ref>{{cite news |title=Knighthood for treble-winner Ferguson |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/special_report/1999/06/99/queens_birthday_honours/366908.stm |work=BBC News |date=12 June 1999 |access-date=19 June 2015}}</ref> as well as a bonus from the club reported to be in the region of £350,000 (the players received bonuses of £150,000 each).<ref name="Ferris"/>{{rp|p=331}} In a post-match interview with [[ITV (TV channel)|ITV]]'s [[Gary Newbon]], Ferguson provided a succinct summary of the game: "Football, eh? Bloody hell".<ref name="Ferris"/>{{rp|p=394}} After the treble was secured, much debate arose among English football fans as to whether the 1999 Manchester United team was the greatest club side ever, alongside past European Cup-winning teams.<ref>{{cite news |title=Are Man Utd the best ever club side? |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/talking_point/353585.stm |work=BBC News |date=4 June 1999 |access-date=7 July 2012}}</ref> It was such a significant achievement for an English football club that [[Prime Minister of the United Kingdom|Prime Minister]] [[Tony Blair]] took the time to watch part of the match and congratulate Manchester United from the opening of the new [[National Assembly for Wales]] in Cardiff.<ref name="treble_joy"/>

Manchester United became the first team to win the European Cup or Champions League without being the champions of Europe or champions of their country the preceding season.<ref name="Ferris"/>{{rp|p=354}} They had finished second behind Arsenal in the [[1997–98 FA Premier League]],<ref>{{cite web |title=England 1997/98 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/eng98.html |publisher=Rec.Sports.Soccer Statistics Foundation |date=26 January 2007 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> but had qualified through UEFA's expanded 24-team format, which had been introduced a season earlier.<ref>{{cite book |title=UEFA Champions League Statistics Handbook Season 1996/97 |year=1996 |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |location=Nyon |page=283 |chapter=Season 1997/98}}</ref> Had Bayern won the cup, they would have become the first team to achieve this feat, having also finished second in the Bundesliga to Kaiserslautern the season before.<ref>{{cite web |title=Germany 1997/98 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesd/duit98.html |publisher=Rec.Sports.Soccer Statistics Foundation |date=7 June 2005 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> While Manchester United finished the season as treble champions, Bayern ultimately lost the DFB-Pokal final to Werder Bremen, which meant they finished the season with the Bundesliga title as their only silverware.<ref name="sky_treble"/>

Manchester United received £2&nbsp;million in prize money from UEFA for winning the competition, on top of the £10&nbsp;million they had already won just for reaching the final; by comparison, Bayern Munich received £1.6&nbsp;million for finishing as runners-up.<ref>{{cite news |title=Gold Trafford |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/business/the_company_file/325977.stm |work=BBC News |date=22 April 1999 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> Manchester United also received another financial boost in the form of an increase in the share price of their parent company on the [[London Stock Exchange]]; following the match, the price rose from 187 pence per share to 190 pence; however, this was still around 50p less than the shares were trading for earlier in the season during the attempted buyout of the club by [[Sky UK|BSkyB]].<ref>{{cite news |title=City's muted salute to United |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/business/the_company_file/354121.stm |work=BBC News |date=27 May 1999 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref>

[[File:Manchester United Treble celebration.jpg|thumb|Manchester United celebrated the treble with an open-top bus down Deansgate in Manchester.]]

Manchester United planned an open-top bus tour of the city of Manchester the day after the game, at which they would parade their three trophies for an expected 700,000 people lining the streets between [[Sale, Greater Manchester|Sale]] and the city centre. The parade culminated with a reception at the [[Manchester Arena]], for which 17,500 tickets were made available at £3 each; proceeds went to Ferguson's testimonial fund.<ref>{{cite news |title=Manchester plans huge victory party |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/354082.stm |work=BBC News |date=27 May 1999 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> Greater Manchester Police later estimated that the actual number of people who attended the parade was around 500,000.<ref>{{cite news |first=Lee |last=Swettenham |title=Manchester United Treble 1999: 15 years on, 30 stunning pictures as 500k Reds watched victory parade |url=http://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/manchester-united-treble-1999-pictures-7176059 |work=Manchester Evening News |date=27 May 2014 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref>

United took UEFA's berth in the [[1999 Intercontinental Cup]] by virtue of the Champions League win, as well as for the new [[FIFA]]-sanctioned [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship|Club World Championship]] the following season. The club's participation in the latter was controversial because, under pressure from [[the Football Association]], they withdrew from defending their FA Cup title in the [[1999–2000 FA Cup|1999–2000 edition]] for it.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/fa_carling_premiership/381662.stm |title=United pull out of FA Cup |date=30 June 1999 |access-date=25 August 2011 |work=BBC Sport |publisher=BBC }}</ref>

==Broadcasting==
In the United Kingdom, [[ITV Sport]] broadcast the final with commentary from [[Clive Tyldesley]] and [[Ron Atkinson]].<ref>{{cite AV media |people=Tyldesley, Clive; Atkinson, Ron |date=26 May 1999 |title=1999 UEFA Champions League Final |medium=Television production |publisher=ITV}}</ref> The broadcast attracted an average of 15.5&nbsp;million viewers, with a peak audience of 18.8&nbsp;million at 21:30, just as Manchester United scored their two goals.<ref>{{cite news |title=Millions watch United victory |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/entertainment/354290.stm |work=BBC News |date=27 May 1999 |access-date=30 August 2015}}</ref> The climax of the game was voted as the fourth-greatest sporting moment ever by [[Channel 4]] viewers in a 2002 [[100 Greatest (UK TV series)|poll]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.channel4.com/entertainment/tv/microsites/G/greatest_sporting/results.html |title=100 Greatest Sporting Moments – Results |year=2002 |publisher=[[Channel 4]] |location=London|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020204090913/http://www.channel4.com/entertainment/tv/microsites/G/greatest_sporting/results.html|archive-date=4 February 2002|url-status=dead|access-date=6 December 2016}}</ref> [[Alan Green (broadcaster)|Alan Green]] and [[Mark Lawrenson]] commentated the final for [[BBC Radio 5 Live]]. In a 2024 BBC poll celebrating the station's 30th anniversary, listeners ranked it eighth on the list of most defining sporting moments in the station's history.<ref>{{cite AV media |last1=Oldroyd |first1=Eleanor |authorlink1=Eleanor Oldroyd |title=BBC Radio 5 Live's 30 Defining Sporting Moments |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xu_qXfbZVN0 |website=YouTube |date=28 March 2024 |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |access-date=14 May 2024 |time=39:40}}</ref>

In Germany, the match was shown on [[RTL Television]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Champions League |url=http://www.berliner-zeitung.de/archiv/champions-league,10810590,9643688.html |work=Berliner Zeitung |date=26 May 1999 |access-date=30 August 2014 |language=de |archive-date=12 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150512205205/http://www.berliner-zeitung.de/archiv/champions-league,10810590,9643688.html |url-status=dead}}</ref> and was seen by an average of 13.59&nbsp;million people over the course of the broadcast.<ref>{{cite news |title=It can't be – Germany left in tearful disbelief after Munich's collapse |url=http://red11.org/mufc/images/99/ecfinal/pdf/others.pdf |work=Fox Sports |agency=Associated Press |publisher=Red11 |date=27 May 1999 |access-date=30 August 2014}}</ref> In Spain, the match was shown on [[Televisión Española|TVE]].<ref>{{cite news |title=TVE se vuelca en Barcelona para enviar al extranjero la señal del Manchester–Bayern |url=http://hemeroteca.abc.es/nav/Navigate.exe/hemeroteca/madrid/abc/1999/05/26/129.html |work=ABC |format=PDF |date=26 May 1999 |access-date=30 August 2014 |language=es}}</ref> In France, the match was shown on [[TF1]] with commentary by [[Thierry Roland]] and [[Jean-Michel Larqué]], the pairing who had also commentated the [[1998 FIFA World Cup final|1998 World Cup final]] for the same station.<ref>{{cite web |title=Manchester United 2–1 Bayern Munich (Finale Ligue des Champions 1998/1999 – TF1/FR) |trans-title=Manchester United 2–1 Bayern Munich (1999 Champions League Final – TF1/French) |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ImGzD-NMego |website=YouTube |date=15 May 2020 |publisher=TF1 |access-date=14 May 2024 |language=FR}}</ref>

==Treble Reunion match==
On 26 May 2019, Manchester United hosted a reunion match at [[Old Trafford]] against a Bayern Munich Legends team to mark the 20th anniversary of winning the treble and to raise money for the Manchester United Foundation.<ref>{{cite web |title=Treble Reunion |url=http://www.manutd.com/treblereunion |website=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |access-date=8 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190517213518/http://www.manutd.com/treblereunion |archive-date=17 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Most of the Manchester United squad from the 1998–99 season returned for the match,<ref name="reunion_squads">{{cite news |first=Adam |last=Higgins |title=Squads confirmed for Treble Reunion match |url=https://www.manutd.com/en/news/detail/squads-confirmed-for-treble-reunion-match-at-old-trafford |website=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=21 May 2019 |access-date=8 June 2019}}</ref> with the exception of Giggs, who was on coaching duty with the [[Wales national football team|Wales national team]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Katie |last=Newton |title=Class of '92 host kickabout for young school children |url=https://www.manutd.com/en/news/detail/mu-foundation-press-release-about-class-of-92-kickabout |website=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=22 March 2019 |access-date=8 June 2019}}</ref> Despite not having been with Manchester United during the 1998–99 season, [[Karel Poborský]], [[Mikaël Silvestre]] and [[Louis Saha]] completed the squad as regular participants in Manchester United Legends matches.<ref name="reunion_squads"/> Ferguson returned to manage the side, six years after his retirement from full-time management, joined by his 1999 assistant [[Steve McClaren]] and former club captain [[Bryan Robson]] as head coach.<ref name="reunion_squads"/> The Bayern squad featured six players who appeared in the 1999 final – Babbel, Kuffour, Effenberg, Matthäus, Jancker and Fink – as well as striker Élber, who missed the original match through injury.<ref name="reunion_squads"/>

Manchester United won the match 5–0. Solskjær, who had been appointed as manager of Manchester United in December 2018, opened the scoring in the fifth minute, having come on for Cole in the first minute of the match due to Cole's continued struggle with kidney trouble. Yorke made it 2–0 on the half-hour mark, before second-half goals from Butt, Saha and Beckham completed the scoring.<ref>{{cite news |first=Adam |last=Marshall |title=Treble Reunion: United 5 Bayern Munich 0 |url=https://www.manutd.com/en/news/detail/match-report-man-utd-v-bayern-munich-treble-reunion-2019 |website=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=26 May 2019 |access-date=8 June 2019}}</ref> The match was played in front of 61,175 spectators and raised £1.5&nbsp;million for the Manchester United Foundation.<ref>{{cite news |title=Treble Reunion raises huge amount for Foundation |url=https://www.manutd.com/en/news/detail/treble-reunion-raises-over-a-million-pounds-for-the-manchester-united-foundation |website=ManUtd.com |publisher=Manchester United |date=26 May 2019 |access-date=8 June 2019}}</ref>

